$B$ and $C$ have the same immediate predecessor $A$, so both must start at the vertex where $A$ ends.
$D$ needs both $B$ and $C$, so both must also end at the vertex where $D$ starts.
Without a dummy, $B$ and $C$ would be two separate edges joining the same pair of vertices, which a network diagram does not allow.
A dummy is drawn from the end of $B$ to the start of $D$, so that $B$ and $C$ end at different vertices while $D$ still depends on both.
