mastery.solution_text:
$D$ needs $\{A, B\}$ and $E$ needs $\{B, C\}$.
The two lists share $B$ but neither contains the other, so $B$ cannot end at the same vertex as $A$, and it cannot end at the same vertex as $C$ either.
$B$ must therefore end at its own vertex, with one dummy carrying $B$ forward to the vertex where $D$ starts and a second dummy carrying $B$ forward to the vertex where $E$ starts.
No other pair of activities overlaps partially, so two dummy activities is the smallest number possible.
